Description: zink keeps the descriptor binding of a storage buffer's 64-bit, 16-bit and 8-bit views
 get_bo_var() makes the 64-bit (16-bit, 8-bit) view of a shader's storage
 buffers by cloning the 32-bit variable that unbreak_bos() made, then set its
 driver_location to 0. unbreak_bos() had given the 32-bit variable first_ssbo
 (first_ubo for uniform buffers), the lowest buffer the shader uses, and the
 accesses of every view are numbered from first_ssbo. When the shader does not
 use its buffer 0 the 32-bit view began at binding first_ssbo and the 64-bit
 view at binding 0, so a 64-bit atomic or store reached the buffer one place
 below the one the program had bound, and nothing arrived in the right one.
 No error was raised: the descriptor it reached was valid.
 .
 It showed on the RTX 3060 (615.71.09) as a point cloud that loaded and drew
 nothing: its rasterise shader declares ssRGBA, the highest binding, without
 reading it, and zink numbers the blocks from the last binding down, so the
 first buffer used was index 1. The same shader draws on radeonsi, which has
 no such split. The clone now keeps the driver_location it was cloned with.
 .
 Tested by tests/egl-surfaceless-ssbo64.c: red before (four 64-bit atomicMin
 results left at ~0ull), green after, on zink over NVIDIA's Vulkan driver.
 Mesa main (zink_compiler.c, 2026-11-09) has the same line; no issue or merge
 request on gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa names it (searched 2026-11-09).
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-09
Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
---
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_compiler.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_compiler.c
@@ -2288,7 +2288,12 @@
       fields[0].type = type;
       fields[1].type = unsized;
       var->type = glsl_array_type(glsl_struct_type(fields, glsl_get_length(bare_type), "struct", false), array_size, 0);
-      var->data.driver_location = idx;
+      /* the clone keeps the driver_location of the 32-bit variable it was made from: unbreak_bos() gave that one
+       * first_ssbo or first_ubo, the first buffer the shader reads or writes, and the descriptor binding of the
+       * variable and the buffer index of its array start there. A 0 here moved the whole 64-bit, 16-bit or 8-bit
+       * view of the buffers to the binding of buffer 0 while the accesses were numbered from first_ssbo, so they
+       * reached the wrong buffer whenever the shader did not use its first buffer.
+       */
    }
    return var;
 }
